    "text": "Few ideas have captured the urban imagination quite like rewilding. Where once a council would have [[1]] on clearing a derelict railway cutting, it now leaves the brambles to their own devices and waits for the wildlife to follow. The logic is persuasive: cities that set [[2]] space for scrubland, ponds and uncut meadows tend to harbour a surprising diversity of insects, birds and mammals. Yet the movement faces an uphill struggle in neighbourhoods where residents equate neatness with civic pride. Complaints about 'untidy' verges are [[3]] — one London borough received hundreds in a single summer — and officials are understandably wary of being seen to neglect their duties.\n\nEcologists argue that the answer lies in communication rather than compromise. Signs explaining that a patch of long grass is deliberate can [[4]] over sceptical residents, they say. Nor do the benefits [[5]] to wildlife alone. Green corridors cool the surrounding streets in heatwaves, absorb stormwater that would otherwise overwhelm the drains and, according to several studies, improve residents' mental wellbeing. Large-scale schemes, however, remain rare, not [[6]] because land in city centres is so expensive that developers are reluctant to part [[7]] a plot that could house flats. Those who have succeeded tend to get everyone involved from the outset, from schoolchildren to the engineers who maintain the pipes. Whether the approach can be extended to the scale a warming planet demands is still an open question, [[8]] the early evidence is encouraging.",
