    "title": "Why We Put It Off: The Psychology of Procrastination",
    "text": "We tend to put procrastination [[1]] to poor time management, a character flaw to be cured by sheer willpower. Psychologists beg to differ. Studies suggest that putting things off has less to do with laziness than with emotion: the task we shirk is rarely the one that is difficult, [[2]] the one that makes us feel anxious, bored or inadequate. Delay offers immediate relief, and the brain, for which a bird in the hand is always worth two in the bush, takes the short-term reward and [[3]] a blind eye to the long-term cost.\n\nThis helps explain why the advice to get your ducks in a row before starting so often backfires. Sorting a desk or colour-coding a timetable feels productive, yet it can be a sophisticated way of holding off starting the real work. A [[4]] factor is perfectionism: students who dread falling short of their own standards are among the most chronic offenders. Researchers who followed several hundred undergraduates found that those who forgave themselves for an earlier delay were [[5]] likely to put off the next task than those who berated themselves. Self-criticism, it seems, [[6]] the very distress that fuels avoidance.\n\nNone of this means deadlines are irrelevant. A firm external limit can sharpen focus, but it is no substitute [[7]] a sustainable habit. The most reliable remedy appears to be modest. Rather than waiting to feel motivated, set aside ten minutes, tell yourself that is all you have to do, and begin. More often than not, the dread begins to [[8]] off once the first awkward sentence is on the page.",
