    "title": "Fake News, 1500s Style: The Press and Its Rumours",
    "text": "It is tempting to remember the printing press as the great liberator of knowledge, but its first century was every bit as chaotic as our own. By the end of the Middle Ages, printers had discovered that the surest route to profit was not scholarship but sensation. Cheap pamphlets, run off in their thousands, [[1]] to the public appetite for rumours of comets, monstrous births and sinister conspiracies, and readers who took the authority of the page [[2]] granted saw little reason to doubt a text that looked as impressive as any manuscript.\n\nAuthorities were slow to grasp what was happening. When forgeries multiplied, they responded not by educating readers but by [[3]] down on printers. Licensing laws, censorship and bonfires of banned books followed, yet none managed to [[4]] the flow, and magistrates threw out most prosecutions for lack of evidence. [[5]] the contrary, attempts to cover up unwelcome facts frequently [[6]] in a larger audience for them.\n\nWhat eventually checked the flood was not repression but habit. Over generations, readers learned to pick apart a text, asking who had written it and why, and printers began to put their names and addresses on title pages in order to protect their reputations. Reliability became a commercial asset. The lesson, historians suggest, is [[7]] wholly reassuring nor entirely bleak: new technologies can multiply the supply of falsehood faster than the skills needed to detect it can keep [[8]] with it.",
