    "title": "Mapping the Last Frontier: Life at the Bottom of the Sea",
    "text": "For all our talk of conquering space, we know more about the surface of Mars than about the floor of our own oceans. Less than a quarter of the seabed has been mapped in any real detail, and what exploration has taken place has [[1]] up some astonishing finds: hydrothermal vents that support entire ecosystems without a trace of sunlight, fish that generate their own light, and microbes that thrive at temperatures that would kill most organisms. The difficulty is easy to understand. At four thousand metres the pressure is roughly four hundred times that at the surface, and a submersible not designed to stand up [[2]] it will be crushed in an instant. Robots have eased the problem, yet even they struggle: a vehicle can be knocked over by a sudden current, and the footage it gathers must be gone over frame by frame by analysts who may [[3]] weeks on a single dive.\n\nMeanwhile the commercial stakes are rising. Several companies are keen to mine the seabed for the metals used in batteries, and governments are negotiating trade agreements and licensing rules on the assumption that the technology will soon be ready for large-scale use. Scientists urge caution. Their case is [[4]] that mining should be forbidden outright, but that decisions are being taken in the dark. Surveys of the habitats concerned remain [[5]] from complete. Even a [[6]] gain in knowledge, they argue, would make a useful [[7]] to the debate, and might spare the ocean floor damage that can never be repaired. Better, they say, to know what is there before it is gone than to discover too late that nobody ever [[8]] to record it.",
