    "title": "Pennies per Play: Who Really Gets Paid When You Stream?",
    "text": "Streaming has been hailed as the saviour of the music industry, and the figures seem to bear [[1]] that view: global revenues have climbed steadily for years after a long slump. But the picture looks very different from the artist's side. A single play earns a fraction of a penny, and most of that never reaches the performer, since it is divided among labels, distributors, publishers and the platform itself. Unless a song is streamed millions of times, the income it generates is little more than a [[2]].\n\nDefenders of the system beg to differ. Streaming, they point out, has opened up the market to thousands of musicians who would never have found a label, and a catalogue, once uploaded, can keep [[3]] money long after the artist has stopped touring. Critics counter that the economics reward quantity over quality: because a play counts after only thirty seconds, songs have grown shorter, introductions have vanished and albums are crowded with tracks designed to hook the listener at once. Whether this is a contributing factor in the decline of the album, or merely a symptom of changing tastes, remains [[4]] to debate.\n\nSeveral reforms have been floated. One would pay each artist from the subscriptions of their own listeners rather than from a communal pool, so that superstars would no longer take the lion's [[5]]. Another would oblige platforms to be more [[6]] about how payments are calculated. Yet any change faces an uphill struggle, because it requires the consent of everyone involved, and the largest labels have little incentive to [[7]] a system that already serves them well. Even a modest gain for independent artists, campaigners say, would be worth [[8]] for.",
