    "id": "c1-p1-06",
    "title": "Sleepy Teenagers Are Not Lazy: What the Science Says",
    "text": "Generations of parents have assumed that teenagers who sleep until noon are simply idle. Sleep researchers beg to differ. During puberty the body's internal clock shifts, so that adolescents do not feel sleepy until around eleven at night and cannot easily [[1]] asleep before then, however early they go to bed. Yet most schools begin lessons at half past eight or earlier, forcing pupils to function on a deficit that [[2]] over the week.\n\nThe consequences are more serious than a few yawns in the classroom. Tired adolescents struggle to concentrate, are more prone to anxiety and, in some studies, are more likely to be [[3]] in road accidents. Sleep loss is also considered a contributing factor in the rise of low mood among teenagers, although it is hard to [[4]] it from the effects of social media, exams and the other pressures of that age.\n\nPolicy is beginning to catch up. Health policy in several countries now encourages later starts, and the evidence from districts that have tried them is encouraging: in one large-scale study of some eight thousand pupils, attendance rose, grades improved and car crashes among older students fell. Not everyone is persuaded. Head teachers worry about bus timetables, sports facilities that must be shared with after-school clubs and parents' working hours. Such objections stem [[5]] genuine concerns, but they are hardly insuperable. Researchers are cautious about [[6]] too much into the early results, [[7]] the direction of travel is clear. Rather than hold off until every uncertainty has been resolved, they argue, schools should start on the [[8]] of the evidence we already have.",
