    "id": "c1-p1-07",
    "title": "Ink Under Ink: Teaching Machines to Read Lost Manuscripts",
    "text": "For centuries, historians who wished to read a damaged manuscript depended on patience and a good lamp. Fire, damp and the habit of medieval scribes of scraping old texts off parchment to reuse it had left thousands of documents [[1]] to all but the most determined scholars. Machine-learning software is changing that. Trained on thousands of letterforms from known hands, a neural network can pick [[2]] faded strokes that the naked eye misses and suggest what lies beneath a later layer of ink.\n\nThe results can be startling. Researchers recently went over a bundle of charred scrolls that had been written off as hopeless, and the software recovered enough text to identify the author's philosophical school. Sceptics, however, are quick to pick apart such claims. An algorithm, they note, does not read; it predicts, and a plausible reconstruction may owe more to the patterns in its training data than to anything the scribe actually wrote. There is also the danger that a confident result will be used to cover up uncertainty. Editors therefore [[3]] on flagging every machine suggestion as such.\n\n[[4]] a human expert must still decide what the scribe meant, the machine can make a useful contribution by narrowing the options. A manuscript once set aside as hopeless may now find a reader, and the questions it raises will [[5]] scholars busy for years. In the best collaborations the machine proposes and the scholar [[6]]. Seen in that light, the technology is less a replacement for the historian [[7]] a very sharp pair of eyes, and the eyes, [[8]] all, still need a mind behind them.",
