    "id": "c1-p1-08",
    "title": "Slow Travel: Why Fewer Places Might Mean More",
    "text": "Not so long ago, the measure of a good holiday was the number of landmarks one could tick off in a fortnight. Today a growing minority of travellers are turning their [[1]] on that model in favour of 'slow travel': fewer destinations, longer stays and, wherever possible, journeys by train, bike or boat rather than plane. The appeal is partly environmental, since flying accounts [[2]] by far the largest share of a typical tourist's carbon footprint, but it is also a reaction against the packaged itinerary, which can leave visitors with a blur of photographs and little sense of where they have been.\n\nProponents insist that a place reveals itself only to those who are willing to set [[3]] time for it. Lingering over a market lunch, say, or missing the afternoon bus on purpose, can open up conversations that no guidebook anticipates. Critics accuse the movement [[4]] elitism, and there is some truth in the charge: a week-long rail journey is hardly cheap, and not everyone can [[5]] to take a fortnight off work. Yet those who stay put often find that money goes a [[6]] way, since they spend less on transport, eat where locals eat and can throw out the rigid timetable altogether. Local businesses benefit too, and the arrangement works best when everyone involved, from host to guest, is in no hurry.\n\nThe best advice, practitioners say, is to treat the journey as part of the destination rather than a hurdle to be cleared, and never as something to [[7]] off on social media. Delays and missed connections are taken [[8]] their stride; after all, all's well that ends well.",
