    "id": "c1-p2-07",
    "title": "Is Less Really More?",
    "text": "Minimalism, once the preserve of architects and monks, has become a lifestyle of sorts. Its converts pull out drawers crammed with forgotten gadgets, photograph the contents and then, with ruthless satisfaction, give the lot away. Books are donated, wardrobes pared down to a few dozen garments, and entire flats repainted in shades of grey. The more we own, [[1]] more we seem to worry about it; that, at any rate, is the premise on [[2]] the movement rests.\n\nSceptics are quick to point out that the pursuit of simplicity can itself become an expensive habit. Seldom [[3]] a trend that preaches restraint generated so many bestselling books, bespoke storage systems and sleek white sofas. It is hardly surprising that some commentators look down [[4]] the whole enterprise as a luxury for those who can afford to throw things away. A single tidy desk, they observe wryly, has launched a thousand merchandising campaigns.\n\nThat is by [[5]] means the whole story, however. Researchers who followed households attempting to declutter found that, [[6]] their initial reasons for starting, few participants regretted the exercise, and many discovered that freedom from possessions left more room for relationships. What matters, it appears, is not how much one owns [[7]] why one owns it. Anyone [[8]] is fully aware of the motives behind their purchases is far less likely to be swayed by advertising, and probably far less likely to fill a cupboard with things they never use.",
