    "id": "c1-p3-01",
    "title": "Taking a Leaf from Nature's Book",
    "text": "Biomimicry, the practice of borrowing solutions from living organisms, was once dismissed as a charming eccentricity, but it has lately acquired genuine scientific [[1]]. The textbook example is the Japanese high-speed train whose nose was remodelled on the beak of the kingfisher after engineers noticed that the bird dives into water with scarcely a ripple. The redesign produced a train that was both quieter and markedly more economical with energy.\n\nWhat makes nature so rewarding a teacher is the [[2]] stretch of time over which its designs have been tested. Every feather, shell and seed, whatever its primary function, represents millions of years of trial and error, and the failures have simply vanished from the record. The lotus leaf, for instance, owes its extraordinary [[3]] to water to a microscopic surface of waxy bumps, which lets droplets roll off and carry dirt with them. Engineers have since copied the structure to create self-cleaning paints, a feat that would have seemed [[4]] to previous generations. Even software borrows from the living world: programmers who train robots through reinforcement learning are, in effect, imitating an animal that learns by being rewarded.\n\nYet the field has not always lived up to its promise. For decades, the [[5]] of cheap synthetic materials discouraged manufacturers from investing in anything so exotic, and many early attempts proved [[6]], producing elegant prototypes that nobody could afford to make. Sceptics add that nature rarely optimises for a single goal; a termite mound is a compromise between ventilation, temperature and [[7]] against predators. Copying one feature in isolation can therefore mislead. Genuine biomimicry demands a [[8]] of understanding that goes well beyond imitating outward shape.",
