    "id": "c1-p3-02",
    "title": "Maps Never Lie, and Other Fictions",
    "text": "For most of recorded history, a map was less a neutral picture of the world than an argument about it. Medieval cartographers placed Jerusalem at the centre of the page, and few contemporaries would have called this a [[1]]; it expressed what mattered rather than what lay nearby. Later, the portolan charts of Mediterranean sailors served to [[2]] generations of hard-won observation, marking harbours, currents and compass bearings with a precision that scholars on land could scarcely match. Hazards that a careless pilot might pass by unawares were drawn in meticulous detail.\n\nNot every advance was an improvement. A single [[3]] of a place name could send a coastline drifting across the page, and maps of the period remained riddled with [[4]], since information passed from sailor to engraver to printer, acquiring fresh mistakes at every stage.\n\nThe turning point came with the accurate [[5]] of longitude in the eighteenth century, made possible by clocks that kept time at sea. Thereafter, improvements in [[6]] allowed surveyors to plot entire coastlines with confidence, and Mercator's projection, [[7]] the most influential of all, became the default image of the planet. It has not escaped criticism. A common [[8]] of the Mercator map is that Greenland appears as large as Africa, whereas Africa is in fact about fourteen times bigger. The lesson is that every map flatters somebody, and the cartographer's choices deserve as much scrutiny as the coastline itself.",
