    "id": "c1-p3-03",
    "title": "The Trouble with Doing Good",
    "text": "Every summer, thousands of teenagers board planes to spend a few weeks building classrooms or teaching English in villages they will probably never see again. The motive is usually admirable, yet the industry that has grown up around it attracts mounting criticism, and not only from cynics. The most thoughtful organisations now insist on genuine [[1]] with local communities, in which the people who live with a project's consequences decide what it should be.\n\nVolunteering can undoubtedly foster a sense of global [[2]], and it encourages young people to [[3]] with experiences far removed from their own. The danger lies in the temptation to [[4]] hardship, treating it as a character-building adventure for the visitor instead of a daily reality for the host. Researchers warn that local teachers, often struggling with a heavy workload, are rarely consulted, and that the [[5]] of difficult questions is part of the problem: who actually benefits when an untrained teenager replaces a paid local builder?\n\nSome projects have proved [[6]] precisely because they depended on a stream of short-term visitors and a grant that could tide them over for a season; they collapsed the moment funding or enthusiasm dried up. Others were run by well-meaning but [[7]] amateurs, who left behind half-finished walls and disappointed expectations. A growing number of agencies therefore require applicants to train for months beforehand, and favour placements whose [[8]] is measured in a season rather than a fortnight, since trust cannot be built in a week. None of this means that young people should stay at home; it means that goodwill, however sincere, is no substitute for preparation, humility and a willingness to listen.",
