    "id": "c1-p3-04",
    "title": "Sugar Pills and the Mind",
    "text": "The placebo effect is one of the oddest phenomena in medicine: a patient swallows a pill containing nothing but sugar and, to the bewilderment of everyone involved, gets better. Long dismissed as statistical noise, the effect is now known to produce [[1]] changes in the brain, including the release of the body's own painkillers. What remains puzzling is why it works so well for some people and so little for others. The response is by no means [[2]], and researchers have found that the colour, price and even the branding of a tablet can alter its power.\n\nIdeas once written off as [[3]] are now being examined in controlled trials. Scientists suggest, [[4]], that a patient's [[5]] may play a part, as individuals who are more optimistic or more anxious seem to react differently. Others focus on the [[6]] of expectation into physical relief, arguing that the brain, primed by a doctor's reassuring manner, effectively rehearses recovery. The old saying that an apple a day keeps the doctor away reflects the same intuition: belief and routine shape health as much as any chemical does, a view that several trials bear out.\n\nAll this raises an awkward dilemma. Doctors cannot simply prescribe dummy pills, since it would be [[7]] to deceive patients about the treatment they receive. Some trials have, strikingly, given volunteers sugar pills while telling them honestly what they were, and a number still reported relief, though the evidence remains [[8]] and the samples were small. For the moment, the safest conclusion is that the encounter between doctor and patient is itself a form of treatment, and one that medicine would be unwise to neglect.",
