    "id": "c1-p3-05",
    "title": "Cheap Thrills, Costly Clothes",
    "text": "A decade ago, a shopper who bought a new jacket every month would have seemed extravagant; today the behaviour has a name, fast fashion, and an entire business model built around it. Many garments now reach a [[1]] end, worn only a handful of times before being thrown away. The [[2]] of trend-led clothes at remarkably low prices has changed what people expect from a wardrobe, and turned shopping into a form of entertainment. Some commentators regard the system as a textbook case of consumer [[3]] at its most efficient, since the industry's profits depend on persuading buyers that last season's purchases are already out of date.\n\nThe costs are borne elsewhere. Brands that [[4]] speed above all else tend to squeeze their suppliers, and the workers who stitch the clothes rarely share in the profits. Elsewhere in the chain the story is [[5]] bleak: the dyes and finishing chemicals used in cheap production often end up in rivers near the factories, a fact hardly visible to a customer browsing on a phone. Researchers have long pointed out that the [[6]] of garments is not an accident but a design feature: stitching that frays and fabrics that lose their shape discourage repair.\n\nChange is coming slowly. Campaigners are calling for greater [[7]] from the companies that profit from the trade, including honest labels that state where and how items were made. Yet there is little point in stricter rules if shoppers carry on buying clothes that are [[8]] damaged after a few washes. The most sustainable garment, as the saying goes, is the one already in your wardrobe.",
