    "id": "c1-p3-07",
    "title": "The Art of Holding a Room",
    "text": "Ask almost anyone to name their greatest fear and public speaking will appear surprisingly near the top. The terror is rarely about the words themselves. Most people who dread the podium know their subject perfectly well; what undoes them is the [[1]], the dry mouth and racing pulse that convince them everyone can see their panic. Yet audiences are far more forgiving than speakers imagine.\n\nClassical teachers of rhetoric understood that a speech succeeds or fails on more than its content. A dull, [[2]] argument will be forgotten however accurate it may be, whereas a modest idea delivered with conviction can linger. Listeners also absorb the [[3]], the meaning that lies beneath the surface of the words, in tone, pace and silence. A pause, correctly timed, can say more than a paragraph. Careful [[4]] matters too, particularly when addressing an audience whose first language differs from your own.\n\nThe best speakers practise relentlessly yet never sound rehearsed. Rising [[5]] at evening classes in public speaking suggests that people now see this as a learnable craft rather than an inborn gift. What they learn first is to avoid any [[6]] between what they say and how they say it; announcing good news in a flat monotone, for instance, leaves listeners confused. And no amount of polish can disguise [[7]]: audiences are quick to sense when a speaker does not believe their own words. Even the most elegant sentence will be [[8]] if it is delivered at breakneck speed. Slow down, breathe, and let the silence do its work.",
