    "id": "c1-p3-08",
    "title": "A Ticket to the Edge of Space",
    "text": "When the first paying passenger rode to orbit in 2001, space tourism was widely regarded as a stunt for the ultra-rich. Two decades on, it has become an industry. Rocket launch costs have undergone a dramatic [[1]], and rival companies now compete to corner the market in short suborbital hops, in which customers spend a few minutes weightless before dropping back to earth. At one firm, a prize draw produced a handful of [[2]], all of whom trained for weeks before they were allowed to lift off.\n\nSupporters insist that the venture has become a thoroughly [[3]] one, and that it will fund research which governments, constrained by the demands of their political parties and annual budgets, would never undertake. Critics are unconvinced. A single launch burns an enormous quantity of fuel, and the effect on the upper atmosphere is hard to quantify, so it is difficult to describe the activity as [[4]] friendly. And although fares may fall, space will remain [[5]] to all but a wealthy few for the foreseeable future, which highlights a stark [[6]] between those who can leave the planet and those who must live on it.\n\nSafety is a further worry. The [[7]] of early engines caused several fatal accidents, and some experts fear that commercial pressure to fly more often will encourage firms to cut corners. Nevertheless, the sector's momentum seems hard to reverse. The attraction is easy enough to grasp: from orbit, the earth appears [[8]] fragile, a thin blue film against the black, and those who have seen it rarely return unchanged. Whether a few minutes of that view justify the expense is another question, and one that only time will answer.",
