    "id": "c1-p5-01",
    "title": "The Case for Doing Nothing",
    "text": "When Ingrid Halloran tells people that she studies boredom, they tend to look faintly relieved, as though a long-feared dinner-party bore had at last been given a purpose. \"They assume I must spend my days in a state of terminal tedium,\" says the cognitive psychologist, laughing. \"In fact, the opposite is true. Boredom is one of the most productive emotions we have, and we have spent the best part of a century treating it as a defect.\"\n\nHer laboratory at a university in the north of England is, appropriately, rather dull to look at: a windowless room, a table, a pile of plastic cups and a stack of photocopied telephone directories. In one of her best-known experiments, volunteers were asked to copy numbers from a directory for twenty minutes before taking a test of creative thinking, in which they had to list as many uses as possible for a pair of cups. Those who had endured the copying consistently outperformed a control group who had gone straight to the test. \"It wasn't that they were cleverer,\" Halloran says. \"Their minds had simply been given nowhere to go, so they went everywhere.\"\n\nThe explanation, as she sees it, stems from what the brain does when outside demands fall away. Neuroscientists have long known that a network of regions becomes more active, not less, when we are doing nothing in particular; it is thought to be involved in stitching memories together, rehearsing future conversations and imagining situations that have never occurred. Boredom, in this account, is the discomfort that pushes us into that mode. \"It is the mind's equivalent of hunger,\" Halloran suggests. \"Unpleasant, yes, but only because it is telling us that something needs attention.\"\n\nThe difficulty, she argues, is that modern life has all but abolished the conditions in which boredom can do its work. A generation ago, a queue, a train journey or a wet Sunday afternoon obliged people to endure a vacuum for a while; now the vacuum can be filled in under a second. \"Every gap is plugged,\" she says. \"And the trouble with plugging a gap is that you never find out what was going to come out of it.\" Critics might dismiss this as nostalgia, and she concedes that her data cannot prove that smartphones have blunted our imaginations. What they do show is that people who cannot tolerate an empty minute are more likely to act on impulse: in one widely reported study, volunteers left alone with their thoughts preferred to give themselves mild electric shocks rather than sit quietly.\n\nYet she is wary of turning boredom into a virtue to be prescribed, like a vitamin. \"The moment someone sells a 'boredom retreat', I'll lose hope,\" she says. Not all boredom is beneficial. Her team distinguishes between the restless, searching kind and a heavier, helpless variety, in which people feel that nothing could possibly interest them. The latter tends to accompany low mood and is linked with poorer concentration and, among teenagers, with risk-taking.\n\nTeachers, she says, often ask what they should do. Her advice boils down to restraint. When a child complains of having nothing to do, the instinct is to produce a worksheet or a screen. \"Sit through the first ten minutes of grumbling,\" she says. \"That's when the child is working up an idea.\" In her experience, the games that children invent out of nothing are more elaborate, and last longer, than any organised activity on offer, and the parents who have learnt to wait have, almost without exception, stopped dreading the phrase \"I'm bored\".\n\nNot everyone is persuaded. A colleague who studies attention points out that several attempts to repeat the telephone-directory experiment have produced negative results or, at best, modest gains. Halloran does not bristle. \"Good — that is how it should work,\" she says. \"My hunch is that the effect depends on how bored people actually become, and we have been measuring that badly. Until we are better at it, I'd be wary of anybody who promises that boredom will make you a genius.\" Becoming aware of the limits of the evidence, she adds, is part of the pleasure of the job, and her own view of the central point has remained unchanged since her first study: that a mind left idle is rarely empty.\n\nAt the end of our conversation I reach for my phone to check the time, and she raises an eyebrow. Then she smiles. \"Go on,\" she says. \"But notice how you feel first.\" I notice that the sensation, faint and faintly irritating, is exactly the one she has spent twenty years defending.",
