    "title": "From Bleeps to Bruckner",
    "text": "In a concert hall in a provincial city last winter, I watched an audience of several hundred people rise to applaud a piece that lasts barely ninety seconds. It had no development section, no recapitulation and, strictly speaking, no composer in the room. It was the main theme from a platform game released when most of the audience's parents were still at school, and the orchestra played it with complete seriousness.\n\nThe music began life as an afterthought: a few bleeps designed to stop a screen from feeling empty, squeezed through chips that could manage only a handful of voices at a time. Yet those limits proved oddly fertile. A composer who has three or four notes to play at once must make every one of them count, and the best early themes are marvels of economy, melodies so compact that a listener can hum them after a single hearing.\n\nThe rapid growth of concerts devoted to this repertoire since the early 2000s has been, by any measure, remarkable. Several leading orchestras report that their game-music evenings sell out faster than their Beethoven cycles. It would be convenient to say that this has rescued the orchestral world from decline, and some enthusiasts do say so. The truth, as I heard through the grapevine from the manager of a regional orchestra who asked not to be named, is more modest: the takings from a successful game night help to pay for a season of Mahler played to half-empty rows, and have, in at least a few cases, put off the job losses that would otherwise have followed a bad financial year. That is not nothing, but it is not salvation.\n\nMore interesting to me is what has happened to the composing. Orchestral music for games is no longer written for chips but for full ensembles, and the decision makers at the large studios, who commission it, have grown fussy about quality. The result borrows from film scoring yet differs from it in a way that any listener can feel but few can explain. A film score accompanies events that happen once, in a fixed order. A game score must accompany events whose timing is unknown: a player may linger at a doorway for forty seconds or for forty minutes. So the music has to be built from loops, layers and transitions, capable of changing mood without the seam showing. In effect, game composers have had to relearn something that most concert composers have largely abandoned: how to write music that can go on indefinitely without becoming tedious.\n\nOrchestras are discovering that the lesson travels. Several conductors I spoke to said that playing game scores had altered how their musicians listen to one another. One described a rehearsal in which the string section, tired of its fifth run through a looping passage, began spontaneously varying its phrasing so subtly that nobody could say who had started it. \"It was the first time all season,\" he said, \"that I felt they were making the music rather than reproducing it.\"\n\nNone of this will satisfy those who regard the phenomenon as a gimmick, and the sceptics have a case worth stating. A concert that depends on film clips from the game playing on a screen above the stage risks reducing the orchestra to a very expensive soundtrack, the instrumentalists turned into a device for delivering the audience's memories live. Nor do all the arrangements deserve the reverence with which they are received: one medley I sat through was so clumsily stitched together that nobody around me could have minded if it had stopped.\n\nYet I find it hard to be dismissive. The most persuasive argument for the repertoire is not that it brings in new audiences, welcome though they are, but that it reveals how much of what we call \"serious\" music depends on habit. The young man beside me, who told me in the interval that he had never been in a concert hall before, listened to a fugal passage with the absorption that my teenage self reserved for football. If a plumber in a red cap got him through the door, he will, I suspect, stay for the encore.\n\nThe question for orchestras is whether they can resist the temptation to treat this new audience as a market to be exploited and treat it instead as a public to be educated, gently and without condescension. The odds are no better than even. But for the first time in decades, the people in the cheap seats are not on their way out.",
