    "id": "c1-p5-04",
    "title": "The Secret Life of Pavement Trees",
    "text": "The plane tree outside my window has been there longer than the building. It stands in a square of earth no larger than a doormat, hemmed in by granite kerbs, and for most of my adult life I regarded it as a piece of street furniture, in the same category as the bins. It was a botanist, rather than a tree, who corrected me. \"People think a city tree is a country tree that has had bad luck,\" she said. \"It isn't. It is a different organism leading a different life.\"\n\nIts difficulties are easily listed. The soil beneath a pavement is typically compacted almost to the consistency of concrete, short of oxygen and laced with de-icing salt, while the roots, hunting for water, are liable to be hacked back whenever a cable or a drain needs attention. Traffic is a constant menace: vehicles clip branches, and where traffic congestion is severe, fumes and dust coat the leaves and block the pores through which they breathe. That any of these trees reach maturity is, the botanist suggested, more surprising than the fact that so many do not.\n\nWhat interests her more than survival, however, is communication. Beneath the pavement, she explained, a tree's roots are rarely alone. They are threaded with the fine filaments of fungi, which swap minerals gathered from the soil for sugars made in the leaves, and which in some forests appear to link one tree to another. Popular books have described this as a \"wood wide web\", through which a mother tree feeds her seedlings and a stricken one warns its neighbours of danger. It is a beautiful image and, she said with a small grimace, a premature one.\n\nThe evidence for the transfer of resources between trees through fungal links is, she pointed out, much stronger in some settings than in others, and the claim that trees \"talk\", in the sense of sending messages deliberately, goes well beyond anything that has been demonstrated. Several recent reviews have found that the most dramatic claims rest on a handful of experiments in a handful of forests. Whether the picture holds for a lime tree growing out of a city pavement is simply not known. \"I am not saying the network doesn't exist,\" she said. \"I am saying that we should be suspicious of a story that is so very satisfying.\"\n\nIn the city the connections that matter may be of another kind altogether. Where several trees share a continuous strip of soil, rather than being planted in isolated pits, their roots can reach one another, and the benefits are measurable: faster growth, greater resistance to drought, longer lives. In many places the standard specification for a planting pit has remained unchanged for decades, but municipal engineers who have noticed the difference are, the botanist said, quietly rewriting it. A row of trees in a shared trench costs more to install than a row of separate pits, yet it is the cheaper option if the trees would otherwise die at half the age.\n\nBecoming aware of such arithmetic is one thing; getting it acted upon is another. The decision makers who approve street budgets are rarely botanists, and the benefits of a mature tree, which include shade, cooling and absorbed rainwater, are diffuse and slow to appear on a spreadsheet. The costs, by contrast, arrive promptly, in the form of cracked paving and complaints about fallen leaves. \"A tree is the only asset in the municipal accounts that gains value if you simply leave it alone,\" she said. \"That is exactly why nobody can be bothered to defend it.\"\n\nShe is not, though, merely a pessimist. She took me on a walk around the back streets, pausing at a battered oak that had been given a new lease of life when residents persuaded the council to replace its paving with a bed of mulch. Further along the same road stood two younger trees, planted in the same year, one lush and the other barely hanging on. \"Same species, same year, same street,\" she said. \"The only difference is the pit.\"\n\nAs I walked home I did what I had not done in years and looked at my own plane tree properly: the mottled bark, peeling like old wallpaper. It struck me that I had mistaken endurance for indifference. The tree had not been ignoring us all this time; it had simply been getting on with things in conditions that would have defeated most of its relatives, and I could, at the very least, stop leaning my bicycle against it.",
