    "title": "The Homework Dilemma",
    "intro": "You are going to read four extracts from articles in which educators and researchers discuss homework in secondary schools.",
    "stem": "Which writer",
    "sections": [
     {
      "l": "A",
      "h": "Writer A — Dr Marta Quiroga, education researcher",
      "t": "Few debates in education generate as much heat and as little light as the one about homework. When researchers pool the results of dozens of studies, the evidence suggests that, for pupils under sixteen, the link between hours spent on take-home tasks and exam performance is feeble at best, and often vanishes once family background is taken into account. That last point deserves emphasis, because the pupil with a desk, a fast connection and a parent who can explain quadratic equations is not competing on level terms with a classmate who shares a single room with three siblings. What does seem to pay off is a different kind of task altogether: a month-long investigation, chosen by the pupil and presented to the class, which teaches planning and perseverance in a way that worksheets seldom manage. Such work cannot be marked in an evening, admittedly, and teachers will protest that the timetable leaves no room for it. Yet homework is not worthless: the habit of managing one's own time, built up gradually during the teens, is arguably its most lasting dividend, and that alone justifies setting some."
     },
     {
      "l": "B",
      "h": "Writer B — Mr Callum Reid, head of mathematics",
      "t": "I have taught mathematics for twenty-six years, and nothing has persuaded me that pupils can master a subject without practising it away from the teacher's gaze. Retrieval, repetition, the slow ironing out of misconceptions: these happen in the quiet of an evening, not in a bustling classroom, and my own results, year after year, bear it out. Attainment rises where regular practice is expected, and it falls where it is waived. The objection that this penalises the poorly housed strikes me as misdirected. Any sensible school already runs an after-hours study room with a computer and a member of staff, and where it does not, it has dropped the ball, not the pupils. As for the fashionable suggestion that teenagers should be set loose on open-ended projects of their own devising, I find it touching but impractical; the average fifteen-year-old needs the scaffolding of short, graded exercises before independence means anything. Those same exercises, completed daily and without fuss, instil the discipline on which everything else rests, and no amount of enthusiasm can substitute for it."
     },
     {
      "l": "C",
      "h": "Writer C — Prof Imani Okafor, sociologist of education",
      "t": "Much of the argument about homework is conducted at the wrong level. Whether it widens the gap between rich and poor is a question I regard as secondary; a badly conceived task is equally pointless in a mansion and in a one-bedroom flat. What matters is design. Take the common practice of asking pupils to finish a worksheet begun in class: it rewards speed and neatness, and its correlation with improved grades is, by most accounts, remarkably thin. I would go further and say that defenders of nightly work overestimate what it teaches about character. A teenager who copies answers from a friend on the bus is learning to comply, not to organise himself, and compliance is a poor substitute for self-direction. Far better, in my view, to give pupils a genuine problem, such as why a local bus route was cancelled, and let them decide how to investigate it, over weeks, with the teacher as consultant rather than inspector. A case in point is the department that swapped weekly worksheets for a single term-long enquiry: it reported a livelier classroom and, curiously, fewer complaints about workload."
     },
     {
      "l": "D",
      "h": "Writer D — Ms Helena Voss, secondary school principal",
      "t": "After a decade running a large comprehensive, I have come to think the problem is not homework itself but where it takes place. Pupils return from school to wildly different evenings: some to quiet and encouragement, others to a younger sibling's tantrums and a phone that never stops buzzing. Leaving learning to chance in this way is, to my mind, a quiet scandal, and the research I trust does not suggest it repays the unfairness, since extra hours at home show scant effect on results. My solution was blunt. We extended the school day by an hour, filled it with supervised study, and abolished the take-home variety almost entirely. Some colleagues urged me instead to hand pupils ambitious independent projects, but without an adult nearby the very pupils who most need guidance founder, and I would rather they had a teacher at their elbow. What surprised me was the effect on habits: by sitting down to work at the same time each day in the same room, students acquire a routine that most adults would envy, and they do it without anyone's kitchen table being involved."
     }
    ],
