    "id": "c1-p6-02",
    "title": "Is Space Worth the Money?",
    "intro": "You are going to read four extracts from articles in which writers discuss the value of space exploration.",
    "stem": "Which writer",
    "sections": [
     {
      "l": "A",
      "h": "Writer A — Dr Lena Ortiz, planetary geologist",
      "t": "Every rover that has trundled across Mars has delivered more science per dollar than any astronaut could, and without needing food, oxygen or a return ticket. I say this as someone who would give a great deal to see a human footprint on another world, but sentiment is not a budget line. Nor am I persuaded by the habitual claim that the programme pays for itself through spin-offs, such as lightweight materials and medical imaging; most of these would have emerged, sooner and more cheaply, had the money been spent on them directly. Where space earns its keep is in the imagination of the young. Its real value lies in the fact that a single image from a distant moon can send a thirteen-year-old to the library, and a country that neglects that spark will pay for it in a thinner supply of engineers. I am rather less sanguine about the arrival of commercial operators. A firm answerable to shareholders will, sooner or later, favour the profitable orbit over the interesting one, and public agencies should not hand over the keys."
     },
     {
      "l": "B",
      "h": "Writer B — Prof Tomás Brandt, economist",
      "t": "The economics of space exploration are rarely examined with the scepticism applied to other public spending. Advocates point to satellite navigation and memory foam as proof of a hidden dividend, yet the figures behind such claims tend to credit the space budget with inventions that were already under way elsewhere. Add the sums required to keep humans alive in a vacuum, and the case for crewed flight collapses beside that for unmanned probes, which cost a fraction and risk no lives. The notion that launches inspire a generation of scientists is just as shaky: the evidence suggests that enthusiasm for lift-off wears off within weeks and rarely alters what a teenager chooses to study. That leaves the private sector, whose arrival I view with caution rather than hostility. Competition has lowered launch costs, but a market left to itself will treat orbit as a dumping ground, and member states ought to insist on binding rules before a few firms decide who may use the sky."
     },
     {
      "l": "C",
      "h": "Writer C — Dame Priya Raman, former flight engineer",
      "t": "Machines can sample, scan and photograph, but they cannot notice. A geologist standing on a ridge will spot in seconds the oddly layered outcrop that a rover, programmed with yesterday's questions, would roll past for years. That is why I part company with those who treat crewed missions as an expensive vanity. The harder the problem human beings are asked to solve beyond Earth, the more ingenuity it draws from us, and the technologies developed along the way, from water purification to the removal of carbon dioxide from cabin air, have repaid the outlay many times over. I am equally relaxed about the commercial scramble now under way. When entrepreneurs compete to build cheaper rockets, they achieve in a decade what government bureaucracies, hedged in by committees, could not manage in three, and it would be foolish to slow them for the sake of tradition. And if that competition fills classrooms with children who want to build the next vehicle, so much the better; no textbook has ever matched the pull of a launch."
     },
     {
      "l": "D",
      "h": "Writer D — Mr Joaquín Alvarado, aerospace engineer",
      "t": "Critics ask what we have to show for the billions spent on space. I would answer that they are holding it: the phone with a camera sensor, the weather forecast, the insulation in a firefighter's jacket. Technologies refined for orbit reach daily life by routes that nobody could have mapped in advance, which is precisely why the investment cannot be judged by the quarter. For the next decade, though, I would put my money on machines rather than people. A probe can spend years in the outer solar system without complaint, and the funds saved by leaving astronauts at home could pay for five more missions. Handing launches to private companies makes sense in that context, since they have proved better than agencies at keeping track of costs and are willing to take risks that no civil servant would sign off. What I find most heartening is the effect on my students: whenever a new probe reaches its target, applications to our engineering course climb, and I have stopped regarding that as coincidence."
     }
    ],
