    "id": "c1-p6-03",
    "title": "Likes, Filters and the Teenage Mind",
    "intro": "You are going to read four extracts from articles in which experts discuss the effect of social media on teenagers' self-esteem.",
    "stem": "Which writer",
    "sections": [
     {
      "l": "A",
      "h": "Writer A — Dr Naomi Feld, developmental psychologist",
      "t": "The assumption that scrolling erodes a teenager's confidence has hardened into received wisdom, though the studies behind it are mostly correlational. Anxious adolescents spend more time online; it does not follow that the time made them anxious, and when researchers follow the same young people over several years the arrows point in both directions. If social media damages anyone, I suspect it does so indirectly, by squeezing out sleep: a girl watching videos at two in the morning arrives at school depleted and brittle, and it is the exhaustion, not the content, that wears down her self-image. For that reason I would put more faith in boundaries at home and in classroom lessons that teach pupils how recommendation algorithms work than in legislation. The evidence suggests that young people who understand how feeds are engineered treat them with healthy cynicism, and that is a protection no regulator can bring into existence. Platforms, to my mind, make a convenient scapegoat for a generation's wider unease, and demanding product changes from them is largely a distraction."
     },
     {
      "l": "B",
      "h": "Writer B — Mr Rafael Okonkwo, youth worker and author",
      "t": "Spend an afternoon reading the comments beneath a teenager's photograph and the damage becomes easy to understand. Ridicule that once ended at the school gates now follows a child home, arrives in the small hours and is witnessed by hundreds, and it would be perverse to claim that this leaves self-esteem untouched. I have worked with young people for twenty years, and the ones who come to us in distress rarely mention filters or influencers; they talk about the group chat that turned on them. The platforms know this. They have the tools to hide counts of likes by default, to prompt a user before a cruel remark is posted and to make leaving a conversation as simple as joining one, yet such features are rolled out grudgingly, if at all. Redesigning the product is where the responsibility lies. Lessons on online safety are worthwhile, and I would never discourage them, but a fourteen-year-old cannot be taught to shrug off a hundred strangers laughing at her."
     },
     {
      "l": "C",
      "h": "Writer C — Prof Sven Lindahl, social psychologist",
      "t": "Adolescence is a period of ferocious social comparison, and no previous generation has had to conduct it against a backdrop of faces edited to the point of unreality. When a sixteen-year-old measures her ordinary morning against another girl's flawless, ironed-out highlights, she is losing a contest she did not know she had entered. I regard that as the central harm, and it is a causal one: experiments in which volunteers cut back their usage show gains in mood within weeks. Tinkering will not fix it. Platforms have had a decade to make their products kinder and have preferred to grow, so I favour a legal minimum age of sixteen, as we have for alcohol and driving, enforced with real penalties. Media-literacy classes can accompany such a law, since knowing that a picture has been retouched does soften its sting, but they are a supplement, not a solution, and children should not be left to defend themselves against an industry spending billions to hold their attention."
     },
     {
      "l": "D",
      "h": "Writer D — Dr Camille Dubois, behavioural scientist",
      "t": "What troubles me about the current debate is how often it fixates on dramatic episodes while neglecting the slow drip of everyday comparison. A feed is, by its nature, a procession of other people's best moments, and teenagers, who are still forming a picture of who they are, tend to mix up the curated version of their peers with the real thing. Hence the dip in self-worth that so many describe. I am sceptical that classes in digital literacy will alter this: pupils can recite that photographs are retouched and still feel diminished, much as knowing how an illusion works does not stop us seeing it. The remedy is better engineering. Platforms could drop visible follower counts, stop pushing aspirational content to users who linger on it, and let young people switch off recommendations altogether, ideally after a red team has been paid to find out how each feature might hurt a fifteen-year-old. A ban, by contrast, simply sends teenagers to less scrutinised corners of the internet."
     }
    ],
