     "1": "Before: <i>what she had actually captured</i> is explained by the comparison with the old <i>postcard</i> in the hut. After: <i>recorded in the same way</i> leads straight to <i>That search</i> and the <i>archives</i>. Paragraph D (the extra) is about drones and has no link to either.",
     "2": "Before: <i>stand where the photographer had stood</i> is only \"in principle\"; the gap starts <i>Repeating a photograph a century on, however</i>, contrasting it with the real difficulty. After: <i>the vantage point had been located</i> picks up <i>matching skylines</i> and \"slotted into place\".",
     "3": "Before: <i>The pairs she produced are unsettling</i> and her care to avoid <i>exaggeration</i>; <i>Not everyone has welcomed the results, however</i> contrasts with this. After: <i>wary of being cast as a scientist</i> leads to <i>What she can offer</i>, i.e. what a photographer (not a scientist) can offer.",
     "4": "Before: <i>audiences who live close to ice</i>; the gap says <i>One such audience</i> and gives an example. After: <i>Moments like that</i> refers back to the girl's question and the silence.",
     "5": "Before: <i>a quieter kind of photography</i>; the gap says <i>It is also a slower and less glamorous one</i> (one = kind of photography). After: <i>Even so</i> contrasts with the money and editor problems just described.",
     "6": "Before: the question <i>what she hopes her pictures will achieve</i>; the gap answers it (<i>Eventually she says...</i>). After: <i>the approach is working</i> refers to making people <i>curious and a little angry</i>, which the visitors' book proves."
