    "id": "c1-p7-05",
    "title": "Reading the Date off a Wreck",
    "text": "A shipwreck does not come with a date stamped on it. When divers first reported a tangle of timbers on the seabed off the Dalmatian coast, the only certainty was that something had sunk. Nobody could say whether the vessel had gone down in the age of sail or in the age of Rome. A century of silt had half-buried the hull, and the timbers that survived were black and as soft as wet bread. Dating a wreck, as the archaeologists soon learnt, is rarely a matter of one clue; it is a process of narrowing, in which each kind of evidence closes in on the truth from a different direction. The most obvious place to begin is the cargo.\n\n[[1]]\n\nA few decades, however, is not good enough, and cargo has a built-in weakness. Pots may be old when they are loaded: a merchant could happily ship a consignment of jars made a generation earlier, or reuse old ones, so that what the archaeologist dates is the freight, not the voyage. A merchant, after all, would no more throw away a serviceable jar than a shopkeeper would discard a shelf. The team therefore turned to the ship itself.\n\n[[2]]\n\nSure enough, the Dalmatian planks yielded a clear pattern, and it matched a regional sequence built up from oak beams in dated buildings. Cores no wider than a pencil had been taken from six timbers, and the best of them preserved the bark edge, the outermost layer, which is the archaeologist's gold. The trees, the specialists concluded, had been felled in the late 1580s. It was an impressive result, but it answered a slightly different question from the one the team had asked.\n\n[[3]]\n\nIt is here that the smaller finds earn their keep. A coin cannot have been lost before it was minted, so the newest coin in a sealed deposit gives what archaeologists call a terminus post quem: a date before which the wreck cannot have occurred. Lost purses, a pewter plate with a maker's mark and even the pattern of a shoe buckle can all be pressed into service. Plain, humble objects can be as telling as treasure, provided the archaeologist can explain how the deposit came about. None of them is conclusive on its own.\n\n[[4]]\n\nIn the Dalmatian case the clash was a real one. The latest coins on the wreck had been minted in 1603, some fifteen years after the felling date. The team's task was to account for the gap, and several theories were proposed, among them a lengthy refit, a ship kept in service for decades, and a collector's hoard. In the end it was the archives that settled the matter.\n\n[[5]]\n\nSuch luck is exceptional. An archive may survive a fire, a war or a flood; more often it does not. Most wrecks leave no paper trail at all, and the archaeologist must make do with physical clues alone, which is why the care with which they are recorded matters so much. That care is not only a question of scientific method; it is also a matter of protecting the site.\n\n[[6]]\n\nFor the Dalmatian wreck, the date arrived just in time. Within weeks of the announcement the local authority had declared an exclusion zone around the site, and divers may now visit only under licence. A ship that nobody could place in time has at last been fixed in it, and, in a modest way, in the memory of the coast. The ledger, incidentally, still gives no name for the captain.",
    "bank": {
     "A": "Here the timber offered a more exact answer. Every tree adds a ring of growth each year, and the width of each ring reflects that year's weather. By comparing the sequence of wide and narrow rings in a plank with reference sequences from living and ancient trees, specialists can often identify the year in which a tree was felled, and sometimes even the season. The technique, known as dendrochronology, has been used on everything from cathedral roofs to Viking ships.",
     "B": "Radiocarbon dating, the method most people associate with archaeology, measures the slow decay of carbon-14 in organic material such as wood, rope or seeds. It is invaluable for very old wrecks, yet for a ship from the sixteenth or seventeenth century its margin of error spans several decades, which is too imprecise for the questions historians most want to ask. Its accuracy also depends on calibration curves that are themselves revised every few years.",
     "C": "A clerk's entry in a customs ledger, found after months of searching, recorded, in a cramped and hurried hand, the loss of a merchant vessel in the autumn of 1604, together with a cargo of olive oil and tableware that closely resembled what the divers had been raising. The primary cause of the loss, according to the ledger, was a sudden north-easterly gale, which drove the vessel onto a reef that every local pilot knew and few captains respected.",
     "D": "Once a wreck has a date, it acquires a status. Sites more than a hundred years old are protected under many national laws and an international convention, and a firm date is often what turns a pile of timber into protected cultural heritage rather than a free-for-all for souvenir hunters. The archaeologists' work, in other words, is not only academic. Enforcement is another matter, since patrol boats are expensive and the sea is large.",
     "E": "Pottery, in particular, is a gift to the dater. Specialists can often glance at a rim and name a century. Amphorae changed shape in subtle but datable ways from century to century, and many carried stamps naming the workshop or the magistrate in office. Matching the jars on the seabed against catalogued types can place a wreck within a span of a few decades. The method is cheap, quick and gloriously low-tech.",
     "F": "Weighing them against one another is the real work, and it amounts to a form of critical analysis that is as much judgement as measurement. No formula does it for them, and the best teams argue loudly. When the evidence agrees, confidence is high. When it clashes, as when a coin of a later year turns up in an older hull, the archaeologist must ask which clue is most likely to mislead, and why.",
     "G": "Ships were repaired, patched and rebuilt for decades, and a plank cut in one year might have been fitted in another. Cutting and fitting were rarely simultaneous. An old hull can contain several generations of replacement wood, which is why archaeologists look for the outermost surviving rings and for signs of whether a plank is original or a later repair. Even then, a felling date gives only a point from which to start."
    },
